  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals a nearly vertically stacked \nupper low slowly spinning over the MN/WI border. The juxtaposition \nof this feature has placed the UP within the system's dry slot, \nshunting the near record PWAT values north into Canada. A few \nscattered showers are lifting across the UP as weak diurnal \ninstability has managed to build behind the nose of the dry slot. \nOnce these showers lift north, a lull in precip is expected to occur \nthrough the evening. Tonight through Thursday morning, the parent \nclosed upper low begins to open as it is gobbled up by a descending \ntrough out of northern Ontario. This works to translate the \nattendant surface low north and east of Lake Superior, bringing the \nsystem's deformation driven precipitation overhead and introducing \nanother round of rain to the northern tier of the UP. A secondary \nshortwave pinwheels around the ascending trough, digging across the \nUP Thursday through early Friday, reinforcing a shot of cold air \ncharacterized by 850 mb temps falling between -3 to -6C over a ~+9C \nLake Superior sfc temp average. Lake enhanced rain will transition \nto pure lake effect snow as deformation driven precip exits east and \nlake induced delta Ts rise to 13C through Thursday afternoon. This \ntransition will likely occur faster over the western UP where sfc \ntemps fall into the low 30s and the interior remains away from the \nlake modified warmer airmass, but there will likely be a period of \nrain/snow mix as this happens. Further east, lake effect rain may be \nthe only precip type as gusty N to NE winds off the lake keep the \narea warmer. At the same time, the aforementioned cold air advection \nand sharp pressure rises behind the departing low (near 1 mb / hr \npossible in the central lake), will quickly ramp up N to NNE winds \nThursday morning. Gusts between 35-45 mph will be common along the \nshorelines of Lake Superior, gradually falling off from west to east \nthrough Friday morning. The biggest question of this forecast period \nremains \"how much snow\" given this is the first measurable snow \nevent of the season. As the transition to lake effect snow occurs \nThursday afternoon, Bufkit soundings reveal lake induced inversion \nheights peaking between 6-8k ft with good moisture throughout the \nlow levels, however, the lake inversion either stays below the DGZ \nor barely scrapes it for less than 3 hours, pointing toward low SLRs \n(likely 10:1 or less) and overall sloppy snow quality made lower by \ncrystal fracturing in gusty winds. Interestingly, some CAMs reveal a \nLake Nipigon connection band impacting portions of Ontonagon/Gogebic \ncounties that may provide an extra boost to snow production where it \nsets up. Pattern recognition suggests that the central UP highlands \nof Baraga/Marquette county will ultimately be the \"winner\" in terms \nof accumulating snow as precip is largely driven by upsloping. By \nFriday morning, a dusting to maybe an inch of wet snow is most \nlikely across the west away from the lakeshores while the Michigamme \nHighlands tally 1-2\" (50-80% for this solution per REFS and NBM). \nThe 12z REFS PMM paints 2-4\" of snow across the central highlands, \nbut deciding to remain conservative with this snowfall forecast. \nWhile I would love to see the 12z HRRR solution pan out at the \noffice (10\"), thinking the lack of DGZ access and approaching dry \nair eating away lake inversion heights makes that particular \nsolution a bit bullish.\n\nBuilding ridge and local high pressure returns drier and warmer than \nnormal conditions this weekend into early next week.",
